  "citation": {
    "sourceUrl": "https://www.in.gov/dor/files/dn01.pdf",
    "sourceDocument": "Indiana DOR Departmental Notice #1 (R47 / 10-26), effective Oct. 1, 2026 — 2.95% state rate, $1,000 exemption, and the 92-county tax rate chart",
    "effectiveYear": 2026,
    "dateRetrieved": "2026-10-19",
    "sourceNote": "Indiana's flat adjusted-gross-income tax is on a statutory glide path down (HEA 1001, 2023): the DOR's rates page states it directly — 'The Indiana Individual adjusted gross income tax rate for 2026 is 2.95% and will adjust in 2027 to 2.90%.' (2025 was 3.00%.) There is no Indiana standard deduction; the relief is a $1,000-per-taxpayer personal exemption (IC 6-3-1-3.5), so $1,000 single and head of household, $2,000 married filing jointly. On top of the state rate every one of Indiana's 92 COUNTIES levies its own income tax on residents — mandatory, not opt-in, and set by the county you lived in on Jan. 1 (IC 6-3.6-8-2) — so it is modeled with the same residence-based single-select the engine uses for Maryland's counties. All 92 rates are read from the Indiana DOR's Departmental Notice #1, 'Indiana County Tax Rates', and they are not a rounding error: they run from Porter's 0.50% to Randolph's 3.00%, with Cass at 2.95% matching the state rate outright. The base is the same one the state rate uses: Schedule CT-40 line 1 is 'the amount from IT-40, line 7' — Indiana taxable income after the personal exemption — multiplied by the county rate, which is exactly what the engine's residence-based local does. Worked example, single $60,000 in Marion County (Indianapolis, 2.02%): taxable = 60,000 − 1,000 = $59,000; state tax = 2.95%·59,000 = $1,740.50; county tax = 2.02%·59,000 = $1,191.80. The default county is Marion, the most populous. Perry County's credit for tax paid to three Kentucky localities (CT-40 lines 5–6) is omitted — it can only reduce the figure, the conservative side. The $1,000 dependent exemption and the additional $1,500 first-year-dependent, $3,000 adopted-child, and age-65/blind exemptions are omitted too, as is the separate county-of-principal-employment rule for filers who lived out of state on Jan. 1. Verified against the Indiana DOR rates page and Departmental Notice #1 (R46 / 01-26, effective Jan. 1, 2026) on 2026-09-02. On Oct. 1, 2026 the DOR reissued the notice (R47 / 10-26, effective Oct. 1, 2026) with one county changed: Boone, 1.70% to 1.71%. That notice is for WITHHOLDING, and this shard is a tax year, so Boone carries the month-weighted rate the DOR's own tax-year chart prints for a mid-year change — (9 x 1.70% + 3 x 1.71%) / 12 = 1.7025% — with both halves recorded in midYearRevision. The rule is read off the 2024 chart, where Fulton, .0268 to .0288 on Oct. 1, 2024, is printed as .0273. The 2026 tax-year chart is not published yet; check Boone against it when it is. Re-checked against R47 on 2026-10-19."
  },
